            <ArticleTitle>Supernumerary phantom limbs in ICU patients with acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1726-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1212/WNL.0000000000002541</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the previously undescribed phenomenon of phantom limb generation in patients with severe acute inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (AIDP).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Between April 2011 and January 2014, we encountered 3 patients with AIDP in our intensive care unit who experienced features of self-limited supernumerary phantom limbs (SPLs) during their course.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The following case series describes the phenomenon of SPLs in AIDP.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This report aims to raise awareness of the possibility of SPLs in the course of AIDP. The pathophysiology and management strategies for this clinical phenomenon are unknown.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2016 American Academy of Neurology.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
